cff-version: 1.2.0
message: If you use this software or its results, please cite it as below.
title: "CognitionBioChem: A structural pharmacology workbench that reports a negative result"
abstract: >-
  A structural pharmacology workbench for cognition-related CNS targets, built around the
  discipline that a displayed number must trace to a computation. Boltz-2 v2.2.1 runs locally
  and produced every predicted structure; chemistry is validated with RDKit; every value
  carries a provenance record, and fields that were never computed render as labels rather
  than figures. Nine studies were pre-registered under content hashes before any data was
  seen, under 27 registered analysis plans counting every superseded version, and their
  artefacts, analysis plans and prediction runs are all under content-addressed custody in the
  repository.

  The headline result is negative and is the reason the project exists in this form. Across a
  screen of 13 candidate-receptor constructs covering 12 distinct peptides - one 41-mer is
  screened against two different receptors, so it is counted twice in every mean and count -
  and a 143-fold full-MSA rerun, designed peptides did not separate from
  composition-matched shuffles of their own amino acids: mean native ipTM 0.629 against a mean
  decoy of 0.628. That hypothesis has been falsified in all eleven retained versions of the two
  screening studies, across candidate sets from six to thirteen constructs.

  A registered positive control then measured the null itself, and narrowed how that negative
  may be read. Sixteen deposited X-ray peptide-receptor complexes were folded against ten
  uniform random permutations of each peptide: the natives beat their own permutations in
  aggregate by +0.0895 ipTM (Holm p = 0.0148, 13 of 16 differences positive), but only 4 of 16
  beat all ten permutations of themselves against a threshold of 5 registered in advance, and
  the natural separation could not be shown to exceed the designed one (Welch p = 0.107). The
  per-candidate reading of the composition-matched null is withdrawn throughout the repository
  as a result: the screen's negative result is a statement about a batch of designs, and the
  null licenses no verdict on any single one. That control is also the only study in the slate
  whose protocol audit records no deviation from its registered plan; the other eight deviated
  in at least one respect and each says so.

  An earlier version of this project displayed fabricated affinity, confidence and safety
  values as though they had been computed; those claims are preserved under `retracted_claims`
  rather than deleted, and the renderers that produced them are gone.
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Seung Ho
    family-names: Jung
    email: seung.jung@gmail.com
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-7914-5306"
version: 1.1.0
# The date this version was PREPARED, not the date it was published: 1.1.0 has no tag and no
# deposit. v1.0.0 was released 2026-08-20 and its date is preserved in the version-DOI entry
# below, where it belongs -- attached to the release it describes. Leaving 2026-08-20 here
# paired "Version 1.1.0" with v1.0.0's release date on the Provenance tab, in both report
# editions and on the deck, all of which interpolate the two side by side.
date-released: "2026-08-23"
# The code is Apache-2.0. The repository also redistributes third-party scientific data
# under the other four, and CC-BY-SA-3.0 is share-alike, so a reuser inherits an
# obligation from it. Listing only Apache-2.0 would understate that. NOTICE says which
# files fall under which.
license:
  - Apache-2.0
  - CC-BY-4.0
  - CC-BY-SA-3.0
  - CC0-1.0
  - MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/hopejsh/CognitionBioChem"
url: "https://github.com/hopejsh/CognitionBioChem"
# Zenodo issues two DOIs. The concept DOI always resolves to the newest release and is what a
# reader normally wants; the version DOI is frozen to one release and is what you cite when the
# exact bytes matter -- a reproducibility statement, for instance.
doi: 10.5281/zenodo.22032684
identifiers:
  - type: doi
    value: 10.5281/zenodo.22032684
    description: >-
      Concept DOI -- always resolves to the latest PUBLISHED release, which today is v1.0.0.
      Version 1.1.0 is not yet deposited: it has no git tag and no version DOI. The most
      recent published release is v1.0.0, and the concept DOI resolves to that record until
      1.1.0 is published.
  # This one must be updated BY HAND at every release: the version DOI is issued by the Zenodo
  # webhook only after the tag is already pushed, so no CI step can know it in advance.
  #
  # It is also the one stamp in this file that must NOT follow `version:` above. A version DOI
  # is frozen to the bytes of one deposit forever, so relabelling this line 1.1.0 would make
  # the record say something Zenodo will never make true. The description read "permanently
  # fixed to v1.0.0 (this release)" while `version:` above read 1.0.0 and the abstract already
  # described the nine-study slate -- so a reader was told to cite v1.0.0 for content v1.0.0
  # does not contain. What was wrong was "(this release)", not "v1.0.0".
  - type: doi
    value: 10.5281/zenodo.22032685
    description: >-
      Version DOI -- permanently fixed to v1.0.0, published 2026-08-20. It is NOT this tree.
      Study #12, interface-null-positive-control-v1, was registered 2026-08-22T06:43:11Z, two
      days after that deposit, and it is what withdrew the per-candidate reading of the
      composition-matched null. The v1.0.0 record therefore describes an eight-study slate
      that does not contain the study which forced this project's central retraction. Cite
      this DOI only to name those exact bytes.
  - type: other
    value: "RRID:SCR_028851"
    description: Research Resource Identifier -- cite this inline in a Methods section
  - type: other
    value: "biotools:cognitionbiochem"
    description: ELIXIR bio.tools registry entry -- https://bio.tools/cognitionbiochem
keywords:
  - structural biology
  - protein structure prediction
  - computational chemistry
  - drug discovery
  - reproducible research
  - research integrity
  - pre-registration
  - negative results
  - provenance
  - peptide design
references:
  - type: software
    title: "Boltz-2"
    notes: >-
      Every predicted structure in this repository was produced by Boltz-2 v2.2.1, whose code
      and weights are MIT-licensed.
    authors:
      - name: "Passaro, S. et al."
  - type: database
    title: "AlphaFold Protein Structure Database"
    notes: >-
      Sixteen deposited models are redistributed here under CC BY 4.0 and compared against
      Boltz-2. AlphaFold Server was not used: its terms prohibit automated use for
      protein-peptide binding prediction.
    authors:
      - name: "Varadi, M. et al."
